    "evidence_text": "Characterization of prostaglandin E2 production by Candida albicans.\n\nBACKGROUND Platelet deposition and aggregation are central to the pathogenesis of ischemic complications of acute coronary syndromes (ACS).\n\nPlasma eptifibatide levels correlated significantly with receptor occupancy but not with inhibition of platelet aggregation.   \n\n\nProstaglandin E2 promotes intestinal tumor growth via DNA methylation\n\nThese findings uncover a previously unrecognized role for PGE(2) in the promotion of tumor progression.\n\nMacrophages produce a large amount of PGE(2) during inflammation.\n\nThis lipid mediator modulates various immune responses.\n\nPGE(2) acts on macrophages and inhibits production of cytokines such as TNF-alpha and IL-12.\n\nHere we identified mPGES as a molecule that is induced by LPS in macrophages.\n\nWe generated mPGES-deficient mice and investigated the role of mPGES in vivo.\n\nThe mice showed no augmentation of the PGE(2) production in response to LPS.\n\nmPGES-1 together with cyclooxygenase-2 were induced in the granulation tissues compared with normal stomach tissues.\n\nThe healing of acetic acid-induced ulcers was significantly delayed in mPGES-1 KO mice compared with WT.\n\nChronic obstructive pulmonary disease and neutrophil infiltration: role of cigarette smoke and cyclooxygenase products.\n\nPGE(2) is produced within human airways, and both pro- and anti-inflammatory activities have been reported.\n\n15-Hydroxyprostaglandin dehydrogenase is down-regulated in colorectal cancer.\n\nCyclic AMP-dependent phosphorylation of thromboxane A(2) receptor-associated Galpha(13).\n\n27%.\n\n19%.\n\nFinally, treatment of (32)P-labeled whole platelets with prostacyclin resulted in a 90 +/-\n\nProstaglandin E2 as an inhibitory modulator of fibrogenesis in human lung allografts.\n\nLR-MSCs from patients with BOS secreted significantly less PGE2 than non-BOS LR-MSCs.",
